---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import WorkDossier from "../components/WorkDossier.astro";
import { projects } from "../data/projects";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import avatar from "../assets/avatar.png";
---

<Layout
  title="Portfolio | Agent Product Engineering"
  description="A single-page portfolio about shipping agent workflows into usable web surfaces, with gateway boundaries, state, streaming recovery, and long-running automation."
  lang="en"
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <div class="lang-switch-row">
      <a class="lang-switch" href="/zh/" title="切换到中文">
        <span class="lang-switch-current">EN</span>
        <span class="lang-switch-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="lang-switch-target">中</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
      <p class="hero-kicker">Hi, I am Zhou Le.</p>
      <h1 id="hero-heading">I build agent workflows that survive the edge between demo and product.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        The newest one is a web agent that speaks for me in recruiting conversations, backed by my
        profile, projects, tools, uploads, session history, and a production gateway.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-supporting">
        Frameworks make the first agent cheap. My work starts where the demo stops: gateway
        boundaries, state, stream recovery, tool isolation, latency, deployment, and feedback from
        people who actually use it.
      </p>
      <p class="status-line">
        <span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        Focused on agent product engineering, not just agent prompts.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-link-row">
        <a href="https://agent.dirtyfancy.sbs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Talk to my web agent
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-bridge" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
      <div class="about-bridge-grid">
        <div class="profile-card">
          <div class="profile-card-frame">
            <Image
              class="profile-card-avatar"
              src={avatar}
              alt="Portrait of Zhou Le"
              width={144}
              height={144}
              loading="eager"
              fetchpriority="high"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="profile-card-copy">
            <p class="profile-card-name">Zhou Le</p>
            <dl class="profile-card-facts">
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Focus</dt>
                <dd>Agent product engineering</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Background</dt>
                <dd>B.Eng. in Software Engineering; M.A. in Translation</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Tools</dt>
                <dd>Codex, Opencode, OpenClaw, ADK</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>GitHub</dt>
                <dd>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://github.com/fancydirty"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    fancydirty
                  </a>
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Social</dt>
                <dd>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/%E4%B9%90-%E5%91%A8-967660354/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    LinkedIn
                  </a>
                  <span class="profile-card-sep">·</span>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://space.bilibili.com/5582337?spm_id_from=333.1007.0.0"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    Bilibili <span class="social-badge">380k</span>
                  </a>
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="about-bridge-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">About Me</p>
        <h2 id="about-heading">I came through language, learned by building, and now focus on agents people can actually use.</h2>
        <p>
          I hold a master's degree in translation from UESTC. Before I started building agent
          workflows, I spent years working with language and content. That background still shapes
          how I build: I care about precision, structure, and whether another person can understand
          what the system is doing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the last year, I have turned that same habit toward AI tooling, coding agents, and
          workflow automation. The work has moved from private automation and content pipelines to
          a recruiting web agent exposed to real users. I still learn by building, but the questions
          are sharper now: who owns the session, what protects the runtime, how does streaming
          recover, and what evidence should the agent use before it answers? Codex, Opencode,
          OpenClaw, and ADK are the tools I return to most.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <WorkDossier projects={projects} />

    <section class="lower-grid">
        <section class="manifesto" aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating Principles</p>
        <h2 id="principles-heading">What the work has taught me so far.</h2>
        <p class="principles-lead">
          I used to think the hard part of agent workflows was writing better prompts. After four systems, including a public-facing recruiting agent, I think the harder line is clearer: knowing an agent framework is different from shipping an agent surface that strangers can safely use.
        </p>
        <ul class="principles-list">
          <li><strong>Frameworks make demos cheap.</strong> ADK can give you a stateful, tool-calling agent quickly. That is useful, but it is only the starting line. The harder work begins when the agent has to face unknown users, uploads, bad traffic, slow tools, and broken streams.</li>
          <li><strong>The gateway is part of the agent.</strong> I do not want to expose an agent runtime directly to the internet. The business gateway owns anonymous sessions, file validation, abuse controls, resume delivery, streaming recovery, and the boundary between public traffic and private tools.</li>
          <li><strong>State should outlive refreshes and crashes.</strong> If a user refreshes the page, or a long run stutters, the system should still know who they are and what already happened. This has held across media tracking, internal ops, content pipelines, and now web agents.</li>
          <li><strong>Latency is product behavior.</strong> A slow first token changes whether the agent feels alive. Moving MCP startup out of the hot path and keeping tools warm mattered as much as prompt wording, because the user experiences the whole system, not just the model.</li>
          <li><strong>Evidence beats fluent answers.</strong> The agent should answer from profile data, project records, uploaded context, and tool results. A polished response is not enough if the system cannot show where its judgment came from.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="now-panel" aria-labelledby="now-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Now</p>
        <h2 id="now-heading">Working on the boundary between agent runtime and product surface.</h2>
        <p>
          Right now I am using a recruiting representative agent as the test case. It has to speak
          with strangers, remember sessions, accept context, use my project evidence, and leave
          useful records for me to review later.
        </p>
        <p>
          The agent itself is no longer the only interesting part. The product boundary around it
          now matters just as much: gateway design, streaming behavior, abuse control, observability,
          and the small fixes that make a public agent feel steady instead of experimental.
        </p>

        <ul class="links-list" aria-label="links">
          <li>
            <a href="mailto:fancydirty@gmail.com">fancydirty@gmail.com</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </section>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <p class="footer-social">
        Also on
        <a href="https://github.com/fancydirty" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/%E4%B9%90-%E5%91%A8-967660354/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://space.bilibili.com/5582337?spm_id_from=333.1007.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Bilibili</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is intentionally small. The goal is to show real systems, modestly and
        clearly.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
